1
00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:02,940
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,180 --> 00:00:03,600
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,800
My name is Catherine and a very Merry Christmas to everyone.

4
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:19,900
That's right, Merry Christmas. We are at that time again where we are celebrating Christmas, we're having dinner with family and friends, we're giving presents and of course, there are some other traditions that come into play like for example Santa Claus.

5
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:20,780
Santa Claus.

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,480
So, we've got a very special advanced level lesson for you today.

7
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:25,280
We've got a poem.

8
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:34,660
It's a very traditional poem that is read or listened to on the holiday and we're going to talk a little bit about some of the interesting words and phrases that are used in this.

9
00:00:34,840 --> 00:00:36,980
But first off, let's just listen to the poem itself.

10
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:53,440
'Twas the night before Christmas, when all through the house, not a creature was stirring, not even a mouse.

11
00:00:54,060 --> 00:00:59,680
The stockings were hung by the chimney with care, in hopes that St. Nicholas soon would be there.

12
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:06,200
The children were nestled all snug in their beds, while visions of sugarplums danced in their heads.

13
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:13,060
And Mama in her kerchief and I in my cap had just settled down for a long winter's nap.

14
00:01:13,780 --> 00:01:19,480
When out on the lawn there arose such a clatter, I sprang from the bed to see what was the matter.

15
00:01:20,140 --> 00:01:25,400
Away to the window I flew like a flash, tore open the shutters and threw open the sash.

16
00:01:25,980 --> 00:01:32,300
The moon on the breast of the new-fallen snow gave the lustre of midday to objects below.

17
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:39,700
When what to my wondering eyes should appear but a miniature sleigh and eight tiny reindeer,

18
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:46,220
with a little old driver so lively and quick, I knew in a moment it must be St. Nick.

19
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:52,880
More rapid than eagles his coursers they came, and he whistled and shouted and called them by name.

20
00:01:53,420 --> 00:02:01,060
Now Dasher, now Dancer, now Prancer and Vixen, on Comet, on Cupid, on Donder and Blitzen!

21
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:07,140
To the top of the porch, to the top of the wall! Now dash away, dash away, dash away all!

22
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:14,320
As dry leaves that before the wild hurricanes fly, when they meet with an obstacle, mount to the sky.

23
00:02:14,980 --> 00:02:21,080
So up to the housetop the coursers they flew with the sleigh full of toys, and St. Nicholas too.

24
00:02:21,920 --> 00:02:27,700
And then in a twinkling I heard on the roof the prancing and pawing of each little hoof.

25
00:02:28,260 --> 00:02:34,460
As I drew in my head and was turning around, down the chimney St. Nicholas came with a bound.

26
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:41,700
He was dressed all in fur from his head to his toe, and his clothes were all tarnished with ashes and soot.

27
00:02:42,180 --> 00:02:47,720
A bundle of toys he had flung on his back, and he looked like a peddler just opening his pack.

28
00:02:48,420 --> 00:02:51,880
His eyes, how they twinkled! His dimples, how merry!

29
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:55,080
His cheeks were like roses, his nose like a cherry.

30
00:02:55,080 --> 00:03:01,200
His droll little mouth was drawn up in a bow, and the beard of his chin was as white as the snow.

31
00:03:02,060 --> 00:03:07,240
The stump of a pipe he held tight in his teeth, and the smoke it encircled his head like a wreath.

32
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:13,000
He had a broad face and a little round belly that shook when he laughed like a bowl full of jelly.

33
00:03:13,420 --> 00:03:19,480
He was chubby and plump, a right old jolly elf, and I laughed when I saw him in spite of myself.

34
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:25,320
A wink of his eye and a twist of his head soon gave me to know I had nothing to dread.

35
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:31,300
He spoke not a word but went straight to work and filled all the stockings, then turned with a jerk.

36
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:36,280
And laying his finger aside of his nose and giving a nod, up the chimney he rose.

37
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:42,820
He sprang to his sleigh, to his team gave a whistle, and away they all flew like the down of a thistle.

38
00:03:43,380 --> 00:03:50,220
But I heard him exclaim ere as he drove out of sight, Happy Christmas to all, and to all a good night!

39
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:58,700
Alright, we're back.

40
00:03:59,020 --> 00:04:01,240
So, the poem is very nice.

41
00:04:01,740 --> 00:04:02,000
It's long.

42
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:09,000
And just to kind of recap what the poem is talking about, what is the general story of the poem?

43
00:04:09,820 --> 00:04:15,000
The story of the poem is that Santa Claus is coming to visit someone's house.

44
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:20,780
And there's a person in the story who is watching Santa Claus arrive.

45
00:04:21,320 --> 00:04:30,000
And he's seeing Santa Claus take the reindeer up onto the roof, come down the chimney, and lay the presents out for the children, filling the stockings as well.

46
00:04:30,340 --> 00:04:34,000
Okay, so it's basically a nice poetic description of Santa Claus.

47
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:41,000
And also, it goes in to describe in a very nice way the way Santa Claus looks like.

48
00:04:41,430 --> 00:04:49,550
It's very descriptive. We have descriptions of what Santa Claus looks like, what he's wearing, his smile, how he's a little bit fat.

49
00:04:50,210 --> 00:04:55,990
We also have descriptions of the animals, the special animals that we hear about so often that are called reindeer.

50
00:04:56,390 --> 00:05:03,000
Alright, so we've divided up the poem by lines, so then it's going to be easier for you to go to the line that we're talking about.

51
00:05:03,730 --> 00:05:08,490
Let's start with the first line, with this first word that's actually very interesting.

52
00:05:09,480 --> 00:05:13,000
"'Twas the night before Christmas when all through the house."

53
00:05:13,420 --> 00:05:14,600
So what does this first word mean?

54
00:05:15,220 --> 00:05:20,420
"'Twas' is an abbreviation, so it's a shortened version of, it was.

55
00:05:21,020 --> 00:05:25,600
Okay, so, but do you actually use this now if you're writing?

56
00:05:26,020 --> 00:05:29,740
No, this is not something I would use now in writing, and it's not something I would say.

57
00:05:30,220 --> 00:05:35,790
It's actually more of an archaic form, something that you might hear in poetry or historical writing.

58
00:05:36,050 --> 00:05:38,870
Okay, so it was the night before Christmas, right?

59
00:05:39,010 --> 00:05:42,410
So we're talking about December 24th.

60
00:05:42,470 --> 00:05:44,750
The night of December 24th, that's right.

61
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Alright, so the first two lines talk about how it was the night before Christmas and in the house there was nothing stirring, not even a mouse.

62
00:05:52,510 --> 00:05:57,850
So not even the smallest little animal, so it's very quiet in the house is what's happening.

63
00:05:58,150 --> 00:06:06,420
So now we jump to line number seven, where the poet describes, "'And Mama in her kerchief and I in my cap.'"

64
00:06:07,060 --> 00:06:13,020
So we know that the person talking is the dad, because he's talking about himself and Mama, mother.

65
00:06:13,420 --> 00:06:14,220
They're in bed together.

66
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:18,040
"'And Mama in her kerchief and I in my cap.'"

67
00:06:18,340 --> 00:06:21,220
So I'm wearing a nightcap, a cover on my head, it's like a hat.

68
00:06:21,960 --> 00:06:22,980
"'And Mama has a kerchief.'"

69
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:24,340
What's a kerchief?

70
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:29,000
A kerchief is another abbreviation, it's short for handkerchief.

71
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:30,000
Okay.

72
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:36,000
And a handkerchief is a piece of fabric or cloth that sometimes we use for our noses when we have to blow our nose.

73
00:06:36,500 --> 00:06:41,320
But also you can use a kerchief in your hair to cover your hair.

74
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:54,000
Okay, so if you can imagine it, this must take place, I don't know, maybe 50, 60 years ago when dads used to wear hats to bed and women also handkerchiefs on their heads.

75
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:59,000
Maybe 200 years ago when people wore these long night hats, nightcaps.

76
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:00,000
The pajamas and everything.

77
00:07:00,380 --> 00:07:11,900
All right, now let's jump over to line 18 and 19 when he's talking about the reindeer and the sleigh arriving and he says, "'I knew in a moment it must be Saint Nick.'"

78
00:07:12,180 --> 00:07:14,000
This is another way of calling Santa Claus, right?

79
00:07:14,340 --> 00:07:18,820
Saint Nicholas is his name, but you also call him Claus or Santa Claus.

80
00:07:19,240 --> 00:07:23,340
Okay, and then he said, "'More rapid than eagles, his coursers they came.'"

81
00:07:23,700 --> 00:07:25,600
So the word order is a bit strange here.

82
00:07:25,620 --> 00:07:26,640
This is common in poetry.

83
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:31,340
We could also say, "'His coursers came more rapidly than eagles.'

84
00:07:31,740 --> 00:07:32,940
They were very, very fast."

85
00:07:33,420 --> 00:07:37,540
But what are these coursers? We're talking about the animals pulling Santa Claus.

86
00:07:37,720 --> 00:07:38,580
And what are those animals again?

87
00:07:38,920 --> 00:07:39,800
They're called reindeer.

88
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:40,460
Reindeer.

89
00:07:40,620 --> 00:07:42,320
So the reindeer are very, very fast.

90
00:07:42,840 --> 00:07:47,460
Okay, that's right because he's making the comparison about how fast eagles are compared to them.

91
00:07:48,710 --> 00:07:49,650
And we hear their names, right?

92
00:07:50,190 --> 00:07:50,530
Exactly.

93
00:07:50,950 --> 00:07:54,090
He goes on to say, "'Now Dasher, now Dancer, now Prancer.'"

94
00:07:54,750 --> 00:07:58,410
And all of their names, Vixen, Comet, Cupid, Donder, and Blitzen.

95
00:07:58,710 --> 00:07:59,910
All eight reindeer.

96
00:08:00,150 --> 00:08:01,870
Each animal has its own name.

97
00:08:02,430 --> 00:08:03,730
That's right. So all eight reindeer.

98
00:08:03,890 --> 00:08:06,850
Actually, we're missing one though that's very common nowadays.

99
00:08:07,790 --> 00:08:11,000
The reindeer that goes in the front lighting, illuminating the sky.

100
00:08:11,730 --> 00:08:16,000
Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer. You might know the song, "'Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer.'"

101
00:08:17,350 --> 00:08:17,630
That's right.

102
00:08:18,030 --> 00:08:24,000
So there are actually nine reindeer nowadays, but I guess this story was before the Rudolph story came out.

103
00:08:25,850 --> 00:08:30,000
This is interesting because those are the names of all the reindeer.

104
00:08:30,140 --> 00:08:33,000
Now, I'm not sure in which order they are. I don't know who's in the back, who's in the front.

105
00:08:33,180 --> 00:08:38,000
I don't know either, but it's important to know that they're very fast, and they come down, and then they go up.

106
00:08:38,760 --> 00:08:42,840
They have to take Saint Nicholas to the roof of the house, on top of the house.

107
00:08:43,260 --> 00:08:43,620
That's right.

108
00:08:43,820 --> 00:08:47,100
So now he arrives to the top of the house, line 32.

109
00:08:47,180 --> 00:08:51,020
We see, down the chimney, Saint Nicholas came with a bound.

110
00:08:51,840 --> 00:08:53,880
He came quickly, down the chimney, boom.

111
00:08:54,440 --> 00:08:58,920
And at the end, he's all dressed in fur from his head to his foot.

112
00:08:59,060 --> 00:09:01,840
So top to bottom, he has fur on.

113
00:09:02,180 --> 00:09:02,640
That's right.

114
00:09:03,040 --> 00:09:06,080
And I think he's got fur because he lives in the North Pole, right?

115
00:09:06,200 --> 00:09:11,000
That's right. And it's very cold where Santa Claus lives, so he has to wear these clothes that are very warm.

116
00:09:11,340 --> 00:09:11,640
Okay.

117
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:19,740
And obviously, since he is going up and down chimneys in line 34, we see, and his clothes were tarnished with ashes and soot.

118
00:09:20,020 --> 00:09:22,620
So to tarnish or tarnish means dirty.

119
00:09:23,620 --> 00:09:25,320
Something is tarnished if it's dirty.

120
00:09:25,780 --> 00:09:31,840
So his clothes, his fur clothes were dirty with ashes and soot.

121
00:09:32,140 --> 00:09:37,000
This means that, you know, in your fireplace, when the fire is gone, there's some gray and black stuff.

122
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,740
And that's what's on Santa Claus' clothes.

123
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:42,000
Okay, very good.

124
00:09:42,200 --> 00:09:47,510
So, and then we go on to line 35, a bundle of toys he had flung on his back.

125
00:09:47,990 --> 00:09:51,410
And he looked like a peddler just opening his pack.

126
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:53,070
All right, a peddler.

127
00:09:53,190 --> 00:09:56,000
Now, this is not just something we talk about on Christmas.

128
00:09:56,450 --> 00:09:57,910
You know, we have peddlers all year round.

129
00:09:58,070 --> 00:09:59,530
What are peddlers? What do they do?

130
00:09:59,890 --> 00:10:01,910
Basically, they go around selling things.

131
00:10:02,230 --> 00:10:07,860
So maybe you see them in a small market or sometimes in different places and maybe, I don't know, on the streets.

132
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:14,000
You see them selling little things like toys or combs or books, something like that.

133
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:16,480
Oranges, you see fruit peddlers, people who sell fruit.

134
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:18,100
Or you can see those watch peddlers.

135
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:20,200
You know, they say, would you like a Rolex watch?

136
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:21,400
That's a watch peddler.

137
00:10:22,330 --> 00:10:23,590
So that's what they do, right?

138
00:10:23,750 --> 00:10:27,000
They're informal salesmen.

139
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:27,490
They don't have a store.

140
00:10:27,730 --> 00:10:28,410
They work on the street.

141
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
So this is why Santa looks like a peddler.

142
00:10:31,450 --> 00:10:32,370
He isn't in a store.

143
00:10:32,570 --> 00:10:33,150
He has a bag.

144
00:10:33,510 --> 00:10:34,750
And he says, look in my bag.

145
00:10:35,110 --> 00:10:35,690
Right, exactly.

146
00:10:36,630 --> 00:10:41,030
From line 37, we go on to describe the way Santa Claus looks like.

147
00:10:41,190 --> 00:10:44,370
So he says, his eyes, how they twinkled.

148
00:10:44,650 --> 00:10:46,150
His dimples, how merry.

149
00:10:47,080 --> 00:10:50,080
Dimples are those small circles on your cheeks.

150
00:10:51,040 --> 00:10:54,980
So some people, when they smile, they have these small holes or circles.

151
00:10:54,980 --> 00:10:55,660
Those are dimples.

152
00:10:55,840 --> 00:10:56,440
They're very cute.

153
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:57,160
That's right.

154
00:10:57,300 --> 00:10:58,380
So not everyone has them, right?

155
00:10:58,540 --> 00:11:00,620
Some people, when they smile, you can see those dimples.

156
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:01,640
All right.

157
00:11:02,020 --> 00:11:05,000
And his cheeks were like roses, his nose like a cherry.

158
00:11:05,200 --> 00:11:06,640
So we're saying that they're red.

159
00:11:06,880 --> 00:11:07,140
They're red.

160
00:11:07,260 --> 00:11:07,560
They're pink.

161
00:11:08,120 --> 00:11:08,360
Okay.

162
00:11:08,820 --> 00:11:14,310
Now in line 39, he says, his droll little mouth was drawn up like a bow.

163
00:11:14,890 --> 00:11:17,710
Now, droll is a word that some people still use.

164
00:11:17,970 --> 00:11:19,670
It's pretty common in places like England.

165
00:11:20,130 --> 00:11:21,530
We don't use it as much in America.

166
00:11:21,530 --> 00:11:23,210
We say funny instead.

167
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:28,000
But something that is droll, D-R-O-L-L, droll is funny.

168
00:11:28,670 --> 00:11:31,430
So his mouth, his face is funny looking.

169
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
Okay. His face is funny looking. All right.

170
00:11:34,490 --> 00:11:40,000
And going on to line 45, we're describing how he was chubby and plump.

171
00:11:40,290 --> 00:11:42,000
So we're basically saying he was a little bit fat.

172
00:11:42,540 --> 00:11:42,800
Yes.

173
00:11:43,300 --> 00:11:43,600
Okay.

174
00:11:44,100 --> 00:11:47,220
A right jolly old elf.

175
00:11:47,740 --> 00:11:48,460
So what is an elf?

176
00:11:49,180 --> 00:11:51,980
An elf is actually normally we talk about elves.

177
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:57,420
So one elf, two elves as people, little people who help Santa.

178
00:11:57,920 --> 00:12:02,320
They're mythical creatures who live in the North Pole and who make presents.

179
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:07,000
So an elf is a small creature who makes presents.

180
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:08,000
Okay.

181
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:08,940
Or maybe lives in the woods.

182
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
There are many different stories with elves.

183
00:12:11,350 --> 00:12:12,000
Right, right. Okay.

184
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:17,000
And in line 46, we say, and I laughed when I saw him in spite of myself.

185
00:12:17,730 --> 00:12:19,890
What does he mean by in spite of myself?

186
00:12:20,190 --> 00:12:24,650
This is really important and this is something we say in English all the time, in spite of myself.

187
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
So I laughed in spite of myself.

188
00:12:27,630 --> 00:12:29,000
So I didn't want to laugh.

189
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:30,000
But I did.

190
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:30,910
I wanted to be serious.

191
00:12:31,330 --> 00:12:32,130
But I did laugh.

192
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:36,430
So in spite of myself, I didn't want to laugh.

193
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:37,470
I laughed anyway.

194
00:12:38,190 --> 00:12:42,070
So you say in spite of myself when you did the thing you did not want to do.

195
00:12:42,350 --> 00:12:42,710
Okay.

196
00:12:43,530 --> 00:12:51,090
And finishing off, we are going towards the last three lines where we say, and away they all flew like the down of a thistle.

197
00:12:51,370 --> 00:12:51,950
What is a thistle?

198
00:12:52,390 --> 00:12:54,610
They're talking about the part of a flower that's a thistle.

199
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:56,000
Oh, okay.

200
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:00,000
So they just, they all flew away like quickly, rapidly like the little flower.

201
00:13:00,450 --> 00:13:05,000
Well, you know when a flower is sending its seeds out, it's down, it's white.

202
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:11,000
So maybe if you blow on a flower with the white seeds, they blow away.

203
00:13:11,150 --> 00:13:12,270
That's what it looks like.

204
00:13:12,350 --> 00:13:15,550
It looks like the seeds of a flower being blown away.

205
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:17,000
Very easy.

206
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:18,000
Okay.

207
00:13:18,230 --> 00:13:22,580
Line 55 says, but I heard him exclaim, ere he drove out of sight.

208
00:13:23,160 --> 00:13:27,800
So this is an older way of saying, as he drove out of sight.

209
00:13:27,960 --> 00:13:30,920
So as he's flying away, I can't see him anymore.

210
00:13:31,100 --> 00:13:32,100
Almost I can't see him anymore.

211
00:13:33,020 --> 00:13:34,980
He exclaimed something.

212
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:39,000
So ere means while this is happening.

213
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:43,900
Now this is another one of those maybe old English words that we don't use anymore, right?

214
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:46,000
You wouldn't really use ere.

215
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:50,000
Ere I was walking to school, I saw the milkman.

216
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:51,000
No, we don't say this anymore.

217
00:13:51,100 --> 00:13:54,820
But you will find it in poetry and you might also find it in stories.

218
00:13:55,340 --> 00:13:55,520
Okay.

219
00:13:55,880 --> 00:14:03,920
And line 56, he ends and this is very the famous way that this poem ends by saying, Happy Christmas to all and to all a good night.

220
00:14:04,260 --> 00:14:07,000
Happy Christmas to all and to all a good night.

221
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:10,000
Yeah, this is the, and the first line of the poem are the most famous.

222
00:14:10,100 --> 00:14:10,920
Everyone knows these.

223
00:14:11,160 --> 00:14:11,360
Okay.

224
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:12,300
Very good.

225
00:14:12,460 --> 00:14:13,020
So a beautiful poem.

226
00:14:13,360 --> 00:14:15,640
Why don't we listen to it one more time?

227
00:14:25,520 --> 00:14:32,160
'Twas the night before Christmas, when all through the house, not a creature was stirring, not even a mouse.

228
00:14:32,760 --> 00:14:38,360
The stockings were hung by the chimney with care, in hopes that St. Nicholas soon would be there.

229
00:14:38,980 --> 00:14:44,860
The children were nestled all snug in their beds, while visions of sugarplums danced in their heads.

230
00:14:45,360 --> 00:14:51,600
And Mama in her kerchief and I in my cap had just settled down for a long winter's nap.

231
00:14:52,200 --> 00:14:58,120
When out on the lawn there arose such a clatter, I sprang from the bed to see what was the matter.

232
00:14:58,830 --> 00:15:03,770
Away to the window I flew like a flash, tore open the shutters and threw open the sash.

233
00:15:04,460 --> 00:15:10,660
The moon on the breast of the new fallen snow gave the luster of midday to objects below.

234
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:25,000
When what to my wondering eyes should appear but a miniature sleigh, and eight tiny reindeer, with a little old driver, so lively and quick, I knew in a moment it must be St. Nick.

235
00:15:25,580 --> 00:15:31,280
More rapid than eagles his coursers they came, and he whistled and shouted and called them by name.

236
00:15:31,960 --> 00:15:39,400
Now Dasher, now Dancer, now Prancer and Vixen, on Comet, on Cupid, on Donder and Blitzen.

237
00:15:40,160 --> 00:15:45,680
To the top of the porch, to the top of the wall, now dash away, dash away, dash away all.

238
00:15:46,660 --> 00:15:52,740
As dry leaves that before the wild hurricanes fly, when they meet with an obstacle, mount to the sky.

239
00:15:53,420 --> 00:15:59,560
So up to the housetop the coursers they flew with the sleighs full of toys, and St. Nicholas too.

240
00:16:00,630 --> 00:16:06,310
And then, in a twinkling, I heard on the roof, the prancing and pawing of each little hoof.

241
00:16:06,950 --> 00:16:12,930
As I drew in my head and was turning around, down the chimney St. Nicholas came with a bound.

242
00:16:13,690 --> 00:16:20,290
He was dressed all in fur from his head to his toe, and his clothes were all tarnished with ashes and soot.

243
00:16:20,850 --> 00:16:26,210
A bundle of toys he had flung on his back, and he looked like a peddler just opening his pack.

244
00:16:26,790 --> 00:16:30,310
His eyes, how they twinkled! His dimples, how merry!

245
00:16:31,090 --> 00:16:33,490
His cheeks were like roses, his nose like a cherry.

246
00:16:34,210 --> 00:16:39,710
His droll little mouth was drawn up in a bow, and the beard of his chin was as white as the snow.

247
00:16:40,490 --> 00:16:45,690
The stump of a pipe he held tight in his teeth, and the smoke had encircled his head like a wreath.

248
00:16:46,250 --> 00:16:51,430
He had a broad face and a little round belly that shook when he laughed like a bowl full of jelly.

249
00:16:52,090 --> 00:16:57,770
He was chubby and plump, a right old jolly elf, and I laughed when I saw him, in spite of myself.

250
00:16:58,590 --> 00:17:03,810
A wink of his eye and a twist of his head soon gave me to know I had nothing to dread.

251
00:17:04,410 --> 00:17:14,750
He spoke not a word, but went straight to work and filled all the stockings, then turned with a jerk, and laying his finger aside of his nose and giving a nod, up the chimney he rose.

252
00:17:15,370 --> 00:17:21,000
He sprang to his sleigh, to his team gave a whistle, and away they all flew like the down of a thistle.

253
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:31,000
But I heard him exclaim, ere as he drove out of sight, Happy Christmas to all, and to all a good night!

254
00:18:03,490 --> 00:18:08,310
It's very interesting because it's part of the American tradition of Christmas.

255
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:15,000
I think maybe also in other parts of the world the poem might be translated or also might be popular.

256
00:18:15,690 --> 00:18:24,630
But you know, Santa Claus and all these things are traditions that are observed in some countries and it's very interesting to understand them.

257
00:18:24,870 --> 00:18:27,890
Absolutely, and I'm very curious to know about your Christmas traditions.

258
00:18:28,550 --> 00:18:32,690
What do you do in your home country on Christmas Eve or on Christmas Day?

259
00:18:33,170 --> 00:18:36,930
Do you know any poems like this or do you listen to this poem in translation?

260
00:18:37,210 --> 00:18:37,510
Let us know.

261
00:18:37,950 --> 00:18:39,000
Our website is EnglishPod.com.

262
00:18:39,550 --> 00:18:41,910
Alright guys, we'll see you there and have a Merry Christmas.

263
00:18:42,150 --> 00:18:42,610
Merry Christmas.

264
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:50,789
Bye.
